{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 47, :comments ["<b>And when the garment has in it a plague of tzara'at.</b> Plagues of garments and houses without doubt do not come from the side of nature, for there is in them no blood and no moistures that bring about decay or any illness or any wound. And perforce you must say that they come by way of miracle, on the side of punishment. And this is a paradigm even for plagues of the body, that they are outside nature on the side of punishment. Now regarding plagues of houses, the Sages said (Arakhin 16a) that they come on account of stinginess, etc.; but regarding plagues of garments they said nothing at all. And it seems to me to say that they come on account of arrogance, for it is the way of the world to take greatest pride in garments of honor, as in the case of Rabbi Yochanan, who called his garments 'those that honor me' (Shabbat 113a), and as the Sages said (ibid. 119a): 'to sanctify HaShem with honor' — honor Him with clean garments. Therefore the law of the stricken garment is burning by fire, for everyone who exalts himself is judged by fire, as is explained above in parashat Tzav on the verse 'it is the burnt-offering on its hearth,' for thus is the nature of fire to rise upward, like this one who rises in rank, and the law of burning the chametz proves it. And its purification is through washing in water, which points to the trait of humility, for waters descend from a high place to a low place. And therefore Scripture mentioned in the verse only a garment of wool or linen and a leather vessel, and did not mention garments of silk made from tola'at shani (crimson worm), because the tola'at alludes to the trait of humility, and the purification of the metzora by means of hyssop and tola'at shani proves it; therefore the plague is not found in garments of tola'at shani — therefore it says here, 'and behold, the plague has not changed its color and the plague has not spread — it is impure.' And with all kinds of plagues, impurity is only by way of spreading — a hint to banish arrogance entirely, not to stand even in a middling way, neither part of it nor a fraction of it (Sotah 5a); rather one must invert his eye — his haughty eyes — entirely. <b>And when the garment has in it a plague of tzara'at.</b> Plagues of garments and houses without doubt do not come from the side of nature, for there is in them no blood and no moistures that bring about decay or any illness or any wound. And perforce you must say that they come by way of miracle, on the side of punishment. And this is a paradigm even for plagues of the body, that they are outside nature on the side of punishment. Now regarding plagues of houses, the Sages said (Arakhin 16a) that they come on account of stinginess, etc.; but regarding plagues of garments they said nothing at all. And it seems to me to say that they come on account of arrogance, for it is the way of the world to take greatest pride in garments of honor, as in the case of Rabbi Yochanan, who called his garments 'those that honor me' (Shabbat 113a), and as the Sages said (ibid. 119a): 'to sanctify HaShem with honor' — honor Him with clean garments. Therefore the law of the stricken garment is burning by fire, for everyone who exalts himself is judged by fire, as is explained above in parashat Tzav on the verse 'it is the burnt-offering on its hearth,' for thus is the nature of fire to rise upward, like this one who rises in rank, and the law of burning the chametz proves it. And its purification is through washing in water, which points to the trait of humility, for waters descend from a high place to a low place. And therefore Scripture mentioned in the verse only a garment of wool or linen and a leather vessel, and did not mention garments of silk made from tola'at shani (crimson worm), because the tola'at alludes to the trait of humility, and the purification of the metzora by means of hyssop and tola'at shani proves it; therefore the plague is not found in garments of tola'at shani — therefore it says here, 'and behold, the plague has not changed its color and the plague has not spread — it is impure.' And with all kinds of plagues, impurity is only by way of spreading — a hint to banish arrogance entirely, not to stand even in a middling way, neither part of it nor a fraction of it (Sotah 5a); rather one must invert his eye — his haughty eyes — entirely." "And know and understand that three kinds of tzara'at come upon three things, which are coverings for a man — this one atop that one. The first covering is his skin over his flesh. Above his skin, his garments, which are a covering for his skin. Above them, his house, for it is a covering for him to save him from rain and storm. And one from whom every covering has been removed is called perua (disheveled) and megulah (uncovered) — therefore it says (Exodus 32:25): 'And Moses saw the people, that it was perua,' and the Sages interpreted (Yalkut Shimoni, Metzora, remez 5263) that they were stricken with tzara'at, as it says of it 'and his head shall be perua' (Leviticus 13:45). Therefore it mentioned first plagues of his skin, and afterward plagues of his garments, and afterward plagues of his houses — to remove his coverings one by one until he would be perua and megulah entirely. And so too is the view of the midrash that the Yalkut Shimoni brings on the verse 'his head shall be perua,' after the manner of 'and he uncovered the screen of Judah' (Isaiah 22:8) concerning the destruction of the Temple — for in the destruction of the house they became uncovered entirely. However, according to the Sages (Vayikra Rabbah 17:4), the order is reversed, for they said: the Merciful One does not strike the person first, etc.; therefore He first brings a plague upon his house; if he did not repent, He brings it also upon his garments; if he did not repent, He brings it also upon his body, etc. And the reason He mentioned plagues of the body first is because the Holy One, blessed be He, threatens with the ultimate great blow first, just as He said to Pharaoh (Exodus 4:23): 'Behold, I am slaying your firstborn son,' and as Rashi explained there — so too here He mentioned plagues of the body first in order to instill fear in the man from that which he fears most. But in truth the Merciful One never strikes the person first, for He is El compassionate and gracious."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:37:06.794156628Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}